@namespace Ben.Web.Website.Library.Feed

@*
    What stands where the composer stands, for somebody who cannot post yet (item 186).

    The feed is the front door: anyone reads it, and a voice in it is what belonging buys. So the
    slot the composer would occupy is never empty — it says who may write here and offers the two
    doors, rather than leaving a visitor to work out why everyone else has a box and they do not.

    Two audiences, one component. A signed-out visitor needs an account first; a signed-in person
    with no group and no case needs a group or a case. The doors are the same two either way,
    which is why this is one card and not two.
*@

<div class="card mb-3 bv-feed-join" id="feed-join-prompt">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h5 class="card-title mb-1">@Heading</h5>
        <p class="card-text text-secondary small mb-3">@Explanation</p>

        <div class="d-flex flex-wrap gap-2">
            @if (!IsAuthenticated)
            {
                <a class="btn btn-primary btn-sm" href="/signup">Create an account</a>
            }
            <a class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm" href="/find">Find a group near you</a>
            <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm" href="/organizations">Start your own group</a>
            <a class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm" href="/my-requests/new">Request an investigation</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Whether the reader is signed in — decides the heading and whether sign-up is offered.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool IsAuthenticated { get; set; }

    private string Heading => IsAuthenticated
        ? "Join a group to post here"
        : "Read anything. Join to join in.";

    private string Explanation => IsAuthenticated
        ? "Posting, replying and following are for people who belong here — members of an "
          + "investigation group, and clients whose case is being worked. Both doors are open."
        : "Anyone can read the feed. Posting, replying and following come with an account and a "
          + "group — or with a case of your own.";
}
